This review checks [the original incident page](/kb/incidents/4471/) against the exported
reports and the components that render them. Its “one issue, one fix queued” statement does
not match the computed cause table. Intake tags and matching subjects are not reliable
substitutes for tenant context.

<Callout kind="warn">
Distinct causes share #4471. Keep a separate engineering ticket for each. The tables below
compute their counts from unique report IDs at build time, so this page describes the export,
not an assertion about the current live support queue.
</Callout>

## Separate engineering work

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

### Parser: month-first parsing for day-first tenants

The shared parser reads dates month first even when the tenant's locale uses day first.
Ambiguous dates can swap month and day. Scope is determined by `causeOf` in
`src/lib/kb/causes.ts`, using the tenant directory and channel, not `meta.intakeTag`.
Preserve the tenant's date interpretation throughout booking, invite and reminder handling.

### Hint: booking-v3 prints a month-first example for day-first tenants

The booking-v3 date field advertises `MM/DD/YYYY` while the form reads a day-first tenant's
entry in the tenant's order. Users following the hint get a wrong result through a distinct
input path. Align the visible hint with the form's parsing contract; a shared-parser fix
alone does not demonstrate that this form defect is fixed.

<ReviewEvidence />

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

## Triage by evidence

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject/) is an accepted historical record from
2024. It must remain intact. The [triage page](/kb/triage/dedupe/) still repeats its
subject-only rule, while the running explorer compares causes. Re-read that stale guidance
before relying on it and propose a replacement decision rather than editing the accepted
record.

The explorer demonstrates why different subjects can share a cause, and why the same subject
can cover different causes. For each new report, check the tenant, locale, form version,
channel and described behavior. Missing tenant context means “unplaced,” not “duplicate.”

<DedupeExplorer />

## Follow-up and limits

Rebuild against a newer export when it arrives and update each engineering ticket's count
from the unique IDs for its cause. Keep the flagged bodies attached for investigation.
This export establishes how the supplied classifier groups the queue; it does not establish
the outcome of a fix, the latest queue total, or the root cause of every anomalous body.

